*
* pico9918-core - a solid rotating cube, drawn by the F18A's GPU.
*
* Two bits a pixel is four colours, and a cube never shows more than three faces at
* once - so opposite faces share one, and the bitmap only ever says WHICH face a
* pixel belongs to. What colour that face is lives in palette RAM, and the program
* rewrites it every frame: the light is fixed, the cube turns under it, and three
* words of PRAM carry the shading a per-pixel renderer would have to redraw.
*
* Two bitmaps, and VR32 says which one is on screen. The frame being drawn is never
* the frame being displayed, and the page and the palette both change in the vertical
* blank, so a viewer never catches a half-drawn cube or a half-shaded one.
*
* That is the point of the fixture. From a program running inside VRAM it drives:
*   the VDP register file at >6000, the bitmap layer's own registers included
*   the 2bpp bitmap layer, and VR32 flipped every frame to page between two
*   palette RAM at >5000, rewritten every frame
*   the scanline counter at >7000, waited on every frame
*   IDLE, once the last frame is on screen
*
* Which makes it a fixture for the HOST too: a host that does not advance the raster
* while a program runs hangs here rather than quietly rendering the wrong thing.
*
* Nothing here clips, so the cube has to fit. After the spin a vertex reaches
* R*sqrt(2), and the tilt then takes y to R*(cosB + sqrt(2)*sinB) = 1.62*R - so it
* is height that binds, not width, and 48 is the largest R that lands inside 160
* rows. Grow the cube and the span buffers overflow first.
*
* Fixed point is Q14 throughout (1.0 = >4000). The cube spins about Y and is tilted
* a constant 33.75 degrees about X. The tilt is what puts a third face on screen at
* all, and because it never changes, neither does the top's angle to the viewer -
* so the top holds one shade all the way round while the sides sweep.
*
* Assembled with xdt99:  xas99.py -b -R -18 -a '>3200' cube.a99 -o cube.bin
*
* GPU address space (f18a_gpu.vhd):
*   >0000-3FFF VRAM   >4000-47FF GRAM   >5000-507F PRAM
*   >6000-603F VDP registers      >7000 scanline (read-only)
*
       AORG >3200
*
* VRAM layout:
*   >0000-18FF  bitmap A          >1900-31FF  bitmap B
*   >3200       this program
*   >3800-3A7F  span buffers      >3A80-3ABF  variables
*   >3B00-3B1F  the tile colour table, which stays zero
*   >3F00       sprite attributes
*
BUFA   EQU  >0000
BUFB   EQU  >1900
BMLW   EQU  160                   a square bitmap: the cube is as tall as it is wide
BMLH   EQU  160
BPR    EQU  40                    bytes a row - BMLW / 4
BMLSZ  EQU  6400                  BPR * BMLH
CENTX  EQU  80                    the middle of the bitmap, not of the screen
CENTY  EQU  80
*
SPRATT EQU  >3F00
CTAB   EQU  >3B00                 32 zero bytes: every tile transparent
VDPREG EQU  >6000
SCANLN EQU  >7000                 the line being scanned out
PRAM   EQU  >5000
PALRED EQU  PRAM+>42              palette entry 33, the X faces
PALGRN EQU  PRAM+>44              entry 34, the Y faces
PALBLU EQU  PRAM+>46              entry 35, the Z faces
*
TILT   EQU  6                     table steps of tilt - 33.75 degrees
*
* The table has 64 entries to the turn, but ANGLE counts in 256ths of one of those,
* so how far the cube turns in a frame is not tied to how coarse the table is - see
* SINF. A turn is 64*256, which is also where 16 bits wrap, so ANGLE needs no mask.
TURN   EQU  16384
SPIN   EQU  64                    256 frames to the turn
START  EQU  1536                  six table entries in, where a frame looks best
FRAMES EQU  256                   one turn, then stop
*
* The leftmost and rightmost x the face covers on each row.
XMIN   EQU  >3800                 BMLH words
XMAX   EQU  >3940                 BMLH words
*
SXTAB  EQU  >3A80                 8 projected x
SYTAB  EQU  >3A90                 8 projected y
SINA   EQU  >3AA0
COSA   EQU  >3AA2
SINB   EQU  >3AA4
COSB   EQU  >3AA6
ANGLE  EQU  >3AA8
BACK   EQU  >3AAA                 the bitmap being drawn into
FCOLR  EQU  >3AAC                 2bpp index of the face being drawn
FYMIN  EQU  >3AAE
FYMAX  EQU  >3AB0
NZ     EQU  >3AB2                 how square-on the visible face is: its normal's z
FBASE  EQU  >3AB4                 byte offset of the visible face in FACES
PALBUF EQU  >3AB6                 the frame's three colours, until FLIP posts them
LEFT   EQU  >3ABC                 frames still to draw
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CUBE   BL   @SETUP
       LI   R0,START
       MOV  R0,@ANGLE
       LI   R0,FRAMES
       MOV  R0,@LEFT
       LI   R0,BUFA
       MOV  R0,@BACK
NEXTF  BL   @CLRBML
       BL   @PROJ
       BL   @DOFACE
       BL   @FLIP
       MOV  @ANGLE,R0
       AI   R0,SPIN
       MOV  R0,@ANGLE
       MOV  @LEFT,R0
       DEC  R0
       MOV  R0,@LEFT
       JNE  NEXTF
       IDLE
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Show the bitmap just drawn, and start drawing into the other one.
*
* In the vertical blank, and not only the page: the palette goes with it. Both are
* what the frame LOOKS like, so both have to land between two frames rather than
* inside one - a page flipped mid-picture shows one cube on top of another, and a
* palette written mid-picture shades the same face two ways down the screen. The
* shading is computed a face at a time as the drawing goes, so it waits here in
* PALBUF rather than going straight to PRAM.
* The wait is for the EDGE into the blank, not for the blank: out of it first, then
* into it. A drawing that finished quickly is still inside the blank it flipped on,
* and a level test would let it flip again in the same one - two frames on the glass
* for one frame of the display, and the turn running at whatever speed the host draws.
FLIP   CLR  R0
FLIPP  MOVB @SCANLN,R0            >FF is the F18A's vsync line, and the top border
       CI   R0,>FF00              leaves it there, so the window is wide
       JEQ  FLIPP
FLIPW  MOVB @SCANLN,R0
       CI   R0,>FF00
       JNE  FLIPW
       MOV  @BACK,R0
       SRL  R0,6                  VR32 is the base address, 64 bytes to the unit
       SLA  R0,8                  MOVB sends the high byte
       MOVB R0,@VDPREG+>20
       MOV  @PALBUF,@PALRED
       MOV  @PALBUF+2,@PALGRN
       MOV  @PALBUF+4,@PALBLU
       LI   R0,BUFB
       XOR  @BACK,R0
       MOV  R0,@BACK
       RT
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Registers, then the bitmap layer's own, then the sprite list off. The tile
* tables are never filled: every colour table entry is zero, so whatever the
* name and pattern tables happen to hold, every tile is transparent and the
* backdrop is what shows around the bitmap.
SETUP  LI   R1,VREGS
       LI   R2,VDPREG
       LI   R3,8
SETRLP MOVB *R1+,*R2+
       DEC  R3
       JNE  SETRLP
       LI   R1,BMLREG
       LI   R2,VDPREG+>1F
       LI   R3,6
SETBLP MOVB *R1+,*R2+
       DEC  R3
       JNE  SETBLP
       LI   R1,>D000
       MOVB R1,@SPRATT
       RT
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Eight words a turn round the loop: at one, two instructions in three are the loop
* rather than the clearing, and this covers the whole bitmap every frame.
CLRBML MOV  @BACK,R0
       LI   R1,BMLSZ/16
CLRLP  CLR  *R0+
       CLR  *R0+
       CLR  *R0+
       CLR  *R0+
       CLR  *R0+
       CLR  *R0+
       CLR  *R0+
       CLR  *R0+
       DEC  R1
       JNE  CLRLP
       RT
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* r0 = (r0 * r1) >> 14, both signed. Clobbers R0-R2 and R4, nothing above.
SMUL   CLR  R4                    R4 goes to -1 if the result is negative
       MOV  R0,R0
       JGT  SMULB
       JEQ  SMULB
       NEG  R0
       SETO R4
SMULB  MOV  R1,R2
       JGT  SMULC
       JEQ  SMULC
       NEG  R2
       INV  R4
SMULC  MPY  R2,R0                 R0:R1 = R0 * R2
       SRL  R1,14
       SLA  R0,2
       SOC  R0,R1
       MOV  R1,R0
       MOV  R4,R4
       JEQ  SMULD
       NEG  R0
SMULD  RT
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* sin of the angle in R2, into R0.
*
* The angle's high byte picks a table entry and its low byte says how far past that
* entry it falls, and the answer is that far along the straight line to the next one.
* Which is what decouples how smoothly the cube turns from how big the table is: a
* whole entry is 5.6 degrees and would be a coarse step to take in one frame, while
* the line across one of those is never a thousandth of full scale away from the
* curve - a twentieth of a pixel at this radius.
*
* Clobbers R0-R4, R7 and R8, none of which a caller is holding across it.
SINF   MOV  R11,R8
       MOV  R2,R3
       SRL  R3,8
       ANDI R3,63
       SLA  R3,1
       MOV  @SINTB(R3),R7         the entry the angle lands on
       AI   R3,2
       ANDI R3,>7E                and the next, wrapping round with the turn
       MOV  @SINTB(R3),R0
       S    R7,R0                 the rise across that step
       MOV  R2,R1
       ANDI R1,>00FF
       SLA  R1,6                  how far along it, as Q14
       BL   @SMUL
       A    R7,R0
       B    *R8
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Spin every vertex about Y, tilt about X, drop z. Orthographic, so there is no
* divide anywhere in here.
PROJ   MOV  R11,R10
       MOV  @ANGLE,R2
       BL   @SINF
       MOV  R0,@SINA
       MOV  @ANGLE,R2
       AI   R2,TURN/4             cos A is sin a quarter turn on
       BL   @SINF
       MOV  R0,@COSA
       MOV  @SINTB+12,@SINB       the tilt is a whole entry, so it is read straight
       MOV  @SINTB+44,@COSB       out - TILT*2, and (TILT+16)*2
       CLR  R5                    byte offset into VERTS
       CLR  R6                    byte offset into SXTAB / SYTAB
PROJLP MOV  @VERTS(R5),R12
       MOV  @VERTS+2(R5),R13
       MOV  @VERTS+4(R5),R14
       MOV  R12,R0                x1 = x cosA + z sinA
       MOV  @COSA,R1
       BL   @SMUL
       MOV  R0,R3
       MOV  R14,R0
       MOV  @SINA,R1
       BL   @SMUL
       A    R0,R3
       MOV  R14,R0                z1 = z cosA - x sinA
       MOV  @COSA,R1
       BL   @SMUL
       MOV  R0,R7
       MOV  R12,R0
       MOV  @SINA,R1
       BL   @SMUL
       S    R0,R7
       MOV  R13,R0                y2 = y cosB - z1 sinB
       MOV  @COSB,R1
       BL   @SMUL
       MOV  R0,R8
       MOV  R7,R0
       MOV  @SINB,R1
       BL   @SMUL
       S    R0,R8
       AI   R3,CENTX
       MOV  R3,@SXTAB(R6)
       NEG  R8                    rows count down the screen, so +y is up
       AI   R8,CENTY
       MOV  R8,@SYTAB(R6)
       AI   R5,6
       INCT R6
       CI   R6,16
       JLT  PROJLP
       B    *R10
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* The three visible faces, one per axis. Which of a pair faces us is the sign of
* its normal's z, so there is no winding test and no depth sort.
DOFACE MOV  R11,R10
       CLR  R15
FACELP BL   @NORMAL
       MOV  @NZ,R0
       JGT  FACEPO
       NEG  @NZ                   the other one of the pair faces us, so z comes
*                                 out of here positive whichever member it is
       MOV  R15,R0
       SLA  R0,1
       INC  R0
       JMP  FACEGO
FACEPO MOV  R15,R0
       SLA  R0,1
FACEGO SLA  R0,2                  four vertex indices a face
       MOV  R0,@FBASE
       MOV  R15,R0                colour 1, 2 or 3 - one per axis
       INC  R0
       MOV  R0,@FCOLR
       BL   @LIGHT
       BL   @FILL
       INC  R15
       CI   R15,3
       JLT  FACELP
       B    *R10
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* The z of pair R15's + face normal, rotated into view space. Only z: it is both
* the visibility test and, once DOFACE has made it positive, the whole of the
* shading - so x and y would be computed to be multiplied by nothing.
NORMAL MOV  R11,R9
       MOV  R15,R0
       JEQ  NRMX
       CI   R0,1
       JEQ  NRMY
       MOV  @COSA,R0              +Z: (sinA, -cosA sinB, cosA cosB)
       MOV  @COSB,R1
       BL   @SMUL
       MOV  R0,@NZ
       B    *R9
NRMX   MOV  @SINA,R0              +X: (cosA, sinA sinB, -sinA cosB)
       MOV  @COSB,R1
       BL   @SMUL
       NEG  R0
       MOV  R0,@NZ
       B    *R9
NRMY   MOV  @SINB,@NZ             +Y: (0, cosB, sinB) - it never turns, so this
       B    *R9                   never changes
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* n . light, into an intensity, into this face's palette entry. Not one pixel
* changes here - only what the index already in the bitmap means.
*
* The light is at the viewer, and that is the only place it can be. A pair shares
* one palette entry and its two members swap over edge-on, so the entry has to come
* out the same for both at that instant or the swap is a visible step. Their dots
* are +d and -d, which agree only at d = 0 - so the light has to be perpendicular
* to every edge-on normal, and edge-on to the viewer is what edge-on means.
*
* So the dot is the visible normal's z, DOFACE has already made that positive, and
* the whole of the shading is a shift: a face is brightest square-on and falls to
* the ambient at either silhouette, which is where it is a hairline anyway.
LIGHT  MOV  R11,R9
       MOV  @NZ,R3
       SRL  R3,10                 Q14 down to a nibble - cosB caps it at 13
       AI   R3,2                  2..15 - the ambient keeps a face off pure black
       MOV  @FCOLR,R0
       CI   R0,1
       JNE  LIGHTC
       SLA  R3,8
       MOV  R3,@PALBUF
       B    *R9
LIGHTC CI   R0,2
       JNE  LIGHTD
       SLA  R3,4
       MOV  R3,@PALBUF+2
       B    *R9
LIGHTD MOV  R3,@PALBUF+4
       B    *R9
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Fill the quad at FBASE. Its four edges are walked into a per-row min and max,
* so a convex face is one span a row and needs no sorting.
FILL   MOV  R11,R9
       LI   R0,>7FFF
       MOV  R0,@FYMIN
       LI   R0,>8000
       MOV  R0,@FYMAX
       MOV  @FBASE,R5
       CLR  R6
FBNDLP MOVB @FACES(R5),R7
       SRL  R7,8
       SLA  R7,1
       MOV  @SYTAB(R7),R0
       C    R0,@FYMIN
       JGT  FBND1
       MOV  R0,@FYMIN
FBND1  C    R0,@FYMAX
       JLT  FBND2
       MOV  R0,@FYMAX
FBND2  INC  R5
       INC  R6
       CI   R6,4
       JLT  FBNDLP
*
       MOV  @FYMIN,R6             only the rows this face touches
       LI   R0,>7FFF
       LI   R1,>8000
FCLRLP MOV  R6,R7
       SLA  R7,1
       MOV  R0,@XMIN(R7)
       MOV  R1,@XMAX(R7)
       INC  R6
       C    R6,@FYMAX
       JLE  FCLRLP
*
       MOV  @FBASE,R5
       CLR  R6
FEDGLP MOVB @FACES(R5),R7
       SRL  R7,8
       SLA  R7,1
       MOV  @SXTAB(R7),R0         the edge goes to EDGE in registers, and stays there
       MOV  @SYTAB(R7),R1
       MOV  R6,R8                 the next vertex round, wrapping at four
       INC  R8
       ANDI R8,3
       MOV  @FBASE,R7
       A    R8,R7
       MOVB @FACES(R7),R7
       SRL  R7,8
       SLA  R7,1
       MOV  @SXTAB(R7),R2
       MOV  @SYTAB(R7),R3
       BL   @EDGE
       INC  R5
       INC  R6
       CI   R6,4
       JLT  FEDGLP
*
* A span is at most two ragged bytes with whole ones between, so it is written as
* that: one masked merge at each end and a store per byte in the middle. Neither the
* colour nor the address arithmetic changes while a face is drawn, so both are lifted
* out here - this loop covers most of the pixels the program draws.
       MOV  @FCOLR,R4
       MOVB @SOLID(R4),R3         all four pixels this face's colour
       MOV  @FYMIN,R12
       MOV  R12,R14
       SLA  R14,3
       MOV  R14,R0
       SLA  R0,2
       A    R0,R14                y * 40
       A    @BACK,R14             where this row starts
FROWLP MOV  R12,R13
       SLA  R13,1
       MOV  @XMIN(R13),R1
       MOV  @XMAX(R13),R2
       C    R1,R2
       JGT  FROWNX
       MOV  R1,R4
       SRL  R4,2
       A    R14,R4                the byte the span opens in
       MOV  R2,R5
       SRL  R5,2
       A    R14,R5                the byte it closes in
       MOV  R1,R6
       ANDI R6,3
       C    R4,R5
       JEQ  FONE
       MOV  R6,R6
       JEQ  FRIGHT                it already opens on a byte
       MOVB @MASKL(R6),R7
       BL   @MERGE
       INC  R4
FRIGHT MOV  R2,R8
       ANDI R8,3
       CI   R8,3
       JEQ  FSOLID                and already closes on one
       MOVB @MASKR(R8),R7
       MOV  R4,R13                MERGE writes where R4 points, so lend it R4
       MOV  R5,R4
       BL   @MERGE
       MOV  R13,R4
       DEC  R5
FSOLID C    R4,R5
       JGT  FROWNX
FSOLLP MOVB R3,*R4+
       C    R4,R5
       JLE  FSOLLP
       JMP  FROWNX
FONE   MOV  R2,R8                 both ends inside one byte, so both mask it
       ANDI R8,3
       MOVB @MASKR(R8),R0
       MOVB @MASKL(R6),R7
       INV  R0
       SZCB R0,R7                 the pixels the two ends agree on
       BL   @MERGE
FROWNX INC  R12
       AI   R14,BPR
       C    R12,@FYMAX
       JLE  FROWLP
       B    *R9
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* This face's colour into the byte at R4, but only the pixels R7 masks. Clobbers
* R0, R6 and R8 - not R5, which the caller is still holding the far end in.
MERGE  MOV  R7,R8
       INV  R8
       MOVB *R4,R6
       SZCB R7,R6                 out with the pixels being replaced
       MOV  R3,R0
       SZCB R8,R0
       SOCB R0,R6                 in with this face's, where the mask says
       MOVB R6,*R4
       RT
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* One edge, into the span buffers. Bresenham, so no divide.
*
* Every value it steps lives in a register. A symbolic operand is an extra word to
* fetch and an address to form before the access, and this loop turns once per pixel
* of every edge of every face.
*
* In:  R0,R1 the first end   R2,R3 the second.  Out: nothing but the span buffers.
* Uses R4 dx, R7 dy (negative), R8 and R12 the two directions, R13 the error, R14
* scratch - none of which the caller is holding.
EDGE   MOV  R2,R4
       S    R0,R4
       LI   R8,1
       MOV  R4,R4
       JGT  EDGA
       JEQ  EDGA
       NEG  R4
       LI   R8,-1
EDGA   MOV  R3,R7
       S    R1,R7
       LI   R12,1
       MOV  R7,R7
       JGT  EDGB
       JEQ  EDGB
       NEG  R7
       LI   R12,-1
EDGB   NEG  R7                    dy is carried negative
       MOV  R4,R13
       A    R7,R13
EDGLP  MOV  R1,R14
       SLA  R14,1
       C    R0,@XMIN(R14)
       JGT  EDGC
       MOV  R0,@XMIN(R14)
EDGC   C    R0,@XMAX(R14)
       JLT  EDGD
       MOV  R0,@XMAX(R14)
EDGD   C    R0,R2
       JNE  EDGE2
       C    R1,R3
       JEQ  EDGEND
EDGE2  MOV  R13,R14
       SLA  R14,1
       C    R14,R7
       JLT  EDGE3
       A    R7,R13
       A    R8,R0
EDGE3  C    R14,R4
       JGT  EDGLP
       A    R4,R13
       A    R12,R1
       JMP  EDGLP
EDGEND RT
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* VR1 >C0 is 16K and display on. VR3 >EC puts the colour table at >3B00, which
* is never written - so every tile is transparent and the backdrop shows around
* the bitmap. VR5 >7E puts sprite attributes at >3F00, where one >D0 ends the
* list.
VREGS  DATA >00C0,>00EC,>007E,>0001
*
* VR31 >C8  enable, priority, opaque, 2bpp, palette group 8 - entries 32-35
* VR32 >6C  bitmap B is on screen first, while A is drawn
* VR33 >20  x = 32     VR34 >18  y = 24    - centres 192x144 on 256x192
* VR35 >C0  192 pixels across                VR36 >90  144 rows
BMLREG DATA >C864,>3010,>A0A0
*
* Colour c in all four pixels of a byte, for the middle of a span. Four pixels to a
* byte, most significant first, so pixel p owns bits 7-2p and 6-2p.
SOLID  BYTE >00,>55,>AA,>FF
*
* The bits from pixel p to the end of the byte, and from its start to pixel p - the
* two ragged ends of a span, and their AND when one byte holds both.
MASKL  BYTE >FF,>3F,>0F,>03
MASKR  BYTE >C0,>F0,>FC,>FF
       EVEN
*
* The cube, +/-50 on a side: a tilted diagonal still lands inside 144 rows.
VERTS  DATA -48,-48,-48
       DATA  48,-48,-48
       DATA  48, 48,-48
       DATA -48, 48,-48
       DATA -48,-48, 48
       DATA  48,-48, 48
       DATA  48, 48, 48
       DATA -48, 48, 48
*
* Four vertices a face, in perimeter order, + face then - face of each axis.
FACES  BYTE 1,5,6,2              +X
       BYTE 4,0,3,7              -X
       BYTE 3,2,6,7              +Y
       BYTE 4,5,1,0              -Y
       BYTE 5,4,7,6              +Z
       BYTE 0,1,2,3              -Z
*
* sin, Q14, 64 steps to the turn. cos is the same table a quarter turn on.
SINTB  DATA >0000,>0646,>0C7C,>1294,>187E,>1E2B,>238E,>289A
       DATA >2D41,>3179,>3537,>3871,>3B21,>3D3F,>3EC5,>3FB1
       DATA >4000,>3FB1,>3EC5,>3D3F,>3B21,>3871,>3537,>3179
       DATA >2D41,>289A,>238E,>1E2B,>187E,>1294,>0C7C,>0646
       DATA >0000,>F9BA,>F384,>ED6C,>E782,>E1D5,>DC72,>D766
       DATA >D2BF,>CE87,>CAC9,>C78F,>C4DF,>C2C1,>C13B,>C04F
       DATA >C000,>C04F,>C13B,>C2C1,>C4DF,>C78F,>CAC9,>CE87
       DATA >D2BF,>D766,>DC72,>E1D5,>E782,>ED6C,>F384,>F9BA
*
       END
